=== thorctl toolbox (exit 0)
Perform toolbox related tasks

Usage: thorctl toolbox <COMMAND>

Commands:
  import        Import a toolbox into Thorium
  build         Build a toolbox manifest from tool, image, and pipeline manifests
  init          Initialize toolbox, tool, image, or pipeline scaffolding
  export        Export Thorium tools and pipelines into a toolbox directory structure
  remove        Remove a previously imported toolbox from Thorium
  diff          Diff an on-disk toolbox against what a Thorium instance has imported
  build-images  Build (and optionally push) the container images in a toolbox
  convert       Convert a legacy toolbox manifest to the current format
  list          List what a toolbox manifest contains
  validate      Check a toolbox manifest without talking to Thorium
  update        Refresh the items of a toolbox that were imported earlier
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help     Print help
  -V, --version  Print version

=== thorctl toolbox import (exit 0)
Import a toolbox into Thorium

A Thorium toolbox is an external collection of tools, toolsets, agents, workflows, and pipelines
pre-configured and ready to run in Thorium. Legacy toolboxes (images and pipelines) are converted in
memory first, and the issues the conversion finds are reported. Tools are created and their versions
published in dependency order, and importing the same toolbox again changes nothing.

Tool versions are told apart by the hash of their spec. A version that matches a published one is
left alone; one with a new label is published; one whose label is taken by a different spec is
published as LABEL+HASH, since published versions never change. If items already exist and differ
you are asked per item to Apply, Skip, or Quit; this needs a terminal. Use --overwrite to apply
every difference without asking, or --skip-conflicts to leave existing items untouched. Use
--dry-run to see the plan.

Usage: thorctl toolbox import [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox manifest to use: a toolbox.json path, an http(s) URL, or a toolbox directory.
          Format: `path | url | dir`
          
          Legacy toolboxes (images and pipelines) are converted in memory first, exactly as `toolbox
          convert` would, and the conversion's issues are reported.

Options:
      --group-override <GROUP>
          Force every item to be imported into a specific group
          
          The group is created if it doesn't already exist.

  -t, --tools <NAME>
          Only import these tools (and what they need), comma separated
          
          Anything a selected item depends on is imported with it.

  -p, --pipelines <NAME>
          Only import these pipelines (and the tools they need), comma separated

      --only <KIND/NAME>
          Only import this item (and what it needs), written KIND/NAME; repeat for several items
          
          The kind is tool, toolset, agent, workflow, or pipeline.

      --overwrite
          Apply every difference without asking
          
          A changed tool version is published under its label, or under `LABEL+HASH` when the label
          is taken by a different spec, since published versions never change. Toolsets, agents,
          workflows, and pipelines that differ are updated in place.

      --skip-conflicts
          Create what is missing but never change what already exists
          
          Each existing item that differs from the toolbox is left as it is and listed. This is safe
          for non-interactive (CI or agent) imports that must never overwrite local changes.

      --dry-run
          Show what would be created, published, and updated, and write nothing

      --skip-confirm
          Don't ask for confirmation before writing
          
          [alias: --yes]

  -o, --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

      --rollback-on-failure
          Automatically roll back what was applied if the import stops early
          
          Without this flag a session with a terminal is asked, and any other session keeps what was
          applied and reports it.

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to use when reviewing a difference
          
          Accepted for compatibility; differences are decided with Apply, Skip, or Quit.

      --update-network-policy
          Update existing Thorium network policies to match the toolbox
          
          Without this, an existing policy that differs is left untouched and only a warning is
          logged. With it, the toolbox's definition is authoritative: rule/flag differences
          overwrite the policy, and a groups-only difference adds the toolbox's groups to the
          existing policy. Group coverage is only ever added, never removed.
          
          This overwrites cluster network-security state, and it applies WITHOUT a prompt when the
          session can't ask.

      --image-path-prefix <REGISTRY/BASE>
          Target registry base path for images bundled in the toolbox
          
          Only used when the toolbox bundles container images (exported with `--with-images`): each
          tool's container image is rewritten to `<image-path-prefix>/<group>/<name>:<tag>`.
          Importing does not push container images; push them to that registry first.

      --follow-latest
          Make pipeline stages of a legacy toolbox follow their tool's latest version instead of
          pinning the version the legacy pipeline named

      --no-pipelines
          Don't import pipelines

      --strict
          Stop before any write if converting a legacy toolbox raised an issue or the manifest has a
          warning

      --create-groups
          Create the groups the toolbox names that don't exist yet, owned by you

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox build (exit 0)
Build a toolbox manifest from tool, image, and pipeline manifests

Walks the directory containing --config (or --path, if given) for manifest.toml files (tools, base
builds, toolsets, agents, workflows, pipelines, and legacy images) and reads the specs and configs
they point at to produce a toolbox.json file suitable for import into Thorium.

The format follows the manifests: any `type = "tool"`, `"build"`, `"toolset"`, `"agent"`, or
`"workflow"` manifest writes the current format (format 2), and legacy `type = "image"` manifests
alone write the legacy format (format 1) with a hint to convert. Use --format-version to choose, and
--rewrite-manifests to convert a directory of legacy manifests in place.

Usage: thorctl toolbox build [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -c, --config <CONFIG.TOML>
          Path to the toolbox TOML config file
          
          [default: config.toml]

      --use-image-path
          Tag every image with its manifest `image_name` (a repo-style path) as the leaf:
          `<registry>/[prefix/]<image_name>:<version>`
          
          By default images are tagged with the tool `name`
          (`<registry>/[prefix/]<name>:<version>`). Set this to use the path-style `image_name` from
          each manifest instead. No effect on images pinned to an explicit url.

  -o, --output <PATH>
          Output path for the generated toolbox.json
          
          Defaults to a `toolbox.json` beside the --config file (the toolbox root). Overriding this
          only redirects the artifact; it does not change where manifests are crawled from.

      --path <DIR>
          Root directory to walk for tool, image, and pipeline manifests
          
          Defaults to the directory containing --config (the toolbox root). Overriding this only
          changes the crawl root; it does not change where toolbox.json is written.

      --tag-suffix <SUFFIX>
          Append this suffix to every derived image tag's version, baking it into the generated
          toolbox.json (tags and embedded image urls)
          
          Lets a CI/CD feature-branch build produce a toolbox.json that references differentiated
          images (e.g. `:1.0-mybranch`) instead of colliding with the mainline `:1.0`. Only affects
          derived `<registry>/...:<version>` tags; images pinned to an explicit url are left
          untouched. Pass the separator you want (e.g. `-mybranch`).
          
          This is the alternative to `toolbox build-images --tag-suffix`, which suffixes tags at
          build time without touching toolbox.json; use one or the other (build-images won't
          re-apply a suffix a tag already carries).

      --format-version <VERSION>
          The format of the toolbox to write: `1` (the legacy images and pipelines) or `2` (tools,
          toolsets, agents, workflows, pipelines with stage tools, and base builds)
          
          By default the format follows the manifests found: any `type = "tool"`, `"build"`,
          `"toolset"`, `"agent"`, or `"workflow"` manifest, or a pipeline with `[stage_tools]`,
          writes format 2, and legacy `type = "image"` manifests alone write format 1. Format 2 over
          legacy manifests converts them exactly as `toolbox convert` does. Format 1 refuses
          manifests that only format 2 can hold.
          
          [possible values: 1, 2]

      --rewrite-manifests
          Rewrite the legacy image and pipeline manifests in place as tool, build, and stage-tool
          manifests
          
          Each `type = "image"` manifest.toml becomes a `type = "tool"` manifest whose `spec_from`
          names its config file (`config_from`), which now holds the converted spec as JSON; an
          image with `[image_from]` keeps it and leaves its spec's image empty. A build-only image
          (no config) becomes a `type = "build"` manifest, and each pipeline's `[images.*]` tables
          become `[stage_tools.*]` tables. Every replaced file is kept with `.bak` added to its name
          and an existing backup is never replaced. Writes format 2, so it can't be combined with
          `--format-version 1`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox init (exit 0)
Initialize toolbox, tool, image, or pipeline scaffolding

Generate default manifest.toml files with their spec or JSON config. Existing files are skipped
unless --overwrite (per-tool files) or --overwrite-config (config.toml, `init toolbox` only) is
given.

Usage: thorctl toolbox init <COMMAND>

Commands:
  toolbox   Initialize a full toolbox with config.toml, image, and pipeline files
  image     Initialize a single image with a manifest.toml and JSON config
  pipeline  Initialize a single pipeline with a manifest.toml and JSON config
  tool      Initialize a tool, toolset, agent, or workflow with a manifest.toml and a spec file
  help      Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox init toolbox (exit 0)
Initialize a full toolbox with config.toml, image, and pipeline files

Usage: thorctl toolbox init toolbox [OPTIONS] --images <PATH>

Options:
  -i, --images <PATH>
          Image build directories, comma-separated. Format: `path[,path,...]` (each gets a
          manifest.toml + JSON config)
          
          Relative paths are resolved under --toolbox-dir.

  -p, --pipeline <PATH[:IMAGE,...]>
          Pipeline directories, optionally binding images. Format: `path[:image,...]`
          
          Use the optional `[:image,...]` colon suffix to bind specific images, e.g.
          `-p ./pipelines/capa:capa,yara`. Without it, all --images are bound. Repeat -p for
          multiple pipelines. A suffix containing a path separator, or a Windows drive
          prefix like `C:`, is part of the path rather than an image list. Relative paths
          are resolved under --toolbox-dir.

  -g, --group <GROUP>
          Group name to use in generated configs (prompted interactively if omitted)

      --toolbox-dir <DIR>
          Path to the toolbox root directory where config.toml will be created
          
          [default: .]

  -c, --config <CONFIG.TOML>
          Seed the new config.toml from an existing one (name, registry, registries,
          `image_path_prefix`, export paths, `bundled_images`, `[base_image]`) instead of
          --name/--registry
          
          Mutually exclusive with --name, --registry, --image-path, and --pipeline-path.

      --name <NAME>
          Toolbox name for config.toml
          
          [default: "My Toolbox"]

      --registry <REGISTRY>
          Container registry for config.toml, e.g. ghcr.io/org/repo
          
          Optional: when omitted, the toolbox declares no central registry and each image's tag is
          taken from the `image` url in its own config.

      --image-path <DIR>
          Directory (relative to the toolbox root) `export` writes image tool dirs under (default:
          images)
          
          Sets `export_image_path` in config.toml. Must be a relative subpath (no absolute path, no
          `..`). Only affects where `export` places files — `build` still discovers manifests at any
          depth.

      --pipeline-path <DIR>
          Directory (relative to the toolbox root) `export` writes pipeline tool dirs under
          (default: pipelines)
          
          Sets `export_pipeline_path` in config.toml. Must be a relative subpath (no absolute path,
          no `..`).

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to use when filling in configs (defaults to your configured `default_editor`)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Skip interactive prompts and use defaults for all fields
          
          Interactive mode needs a terminal on stdin and stderr; without one, pass -n together with
          --group.

      --overwrite
          Overwrite existing per-tool files instead of skipping them
          
          Covers the scaffolded manifest.toml/JSON/description files. It does NOT touch an existing
          config.toml — use --overwrite-config for that.

      --overwrite-config
          Overwrite an existing config.toml instead of preserving it
          
          By default an existing config.toml is kept (so re-running init in a toolbox doesn't
          clobber its settings). Distinct from --overwrite, which covers per-tool files.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox init image (exit 0)
Initialize a single image with a manifest.toml and JSON config

Usage: thorctl toolbox init image [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          Path to the image build directory (the scaffolded files land here)

Options:
      --image-name <IMAGE_NAME>
          The manifest `image_name`: the registry tag path leaf used at build time, formatted as a
          repo-style path (`<registry>/<image_name>:<version>`); defaults to the build directory
          name

  -g, --group <GROUP>
          Group name to use in the generated image config (prompted interactively if omitted)

  -c, --config <CONFIG.TOML>
          Validate against an existing toolbox's config.toml (resolution source, not placement)
          
          When set, scaffolding errors if an image of the same name+version already exists in that
          toolbox (pass --overwrite to replace). Does not move files — the positional path is the
          destination.

      --no-build
          Skip building this image in CI/CD (image already exists in registry)

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to use when filling in the config (defaults to your configured
          `default_editor`)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Skip interactive prompts and use defaults for all fields
          
          Interactive mode needs a terminal on stdin and stderr; without one, pass -n together with
          --group.

      --overwrite
          Overwrite existing files instead of skipping them

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox init pipeline (exit 0)
Initialize a single pipeline with a manifest.toml and JSON config

Usage: thorctl toolbox init pipeline [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          Path to the pipeline directory (the scaffolded files land here)

Options:
  -i, --images <IMAGE>
          Image names this pipeline runs, comma-separated. Format: `image[,image,...]`
          
          Required with --non-interactive. Otherwise, when omitted, the editor opens with an empty
          order to fill in.

  -g, --group <GROUP>
          Group name to use in the generated pipeline config (prompted interactively if omitted)

      --order <JSON>
          Pipeline order as JSON: a list of parallel stages, e.g. `[["img1","img2"],["img3"]]`, or a
          flat list like `["img1","img2"]` (defaults to all images in a single parallel stage)

  -c, --config <CONFIG.TOML>
          Resolve the pipeline's images against an existing toolbox's config.toml (the "look here"
          source, not placement)
          
          When set, every referenced image must exist in that toolbox (else an error — `init
          pipeline` never creates images), and each is version-pinned from the toolbox instead of
          `latest`. Does not move files — the positional path is the destination.

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to use when filling in the config (defaults to your configured
          `default_editor`)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Skip interactive prompts and use defaults for all fields
          
          Interactive mode needs a terminal on stdin and stderr; without one, pass -n together with
          --group and --images.

      --overwrite
          Overwrite existing files instead of skipping them

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox init tool (exit 0)
Initialize a tool, toolset, agent, or workflow with a manifest.toml and a spec file

Writes a `type = "tool"` (or toolset, agent, workflow) manifest.toml and the spec file it points at:
`<name>.json` for a tool, `<name>.toolset.yaml`, `<name>.agent.yaml`, or `<name>.workflow.yaml` for
the others. Fill in the spec, then run `toolbox build`.

Usage: thorctl toolbox init tool [OPTIONS] --kind <KIND> <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          Path to the directory to scaffold into; its name is the item's name

Options:
      --kind <KIND>
          The kind of item to scaffold

          Possible values:
          - image:    A tool run as Thorium jobs from a container image
          - agentic:  A tool a model calls (backed by Thorium's MCP server, a container tool, or a
            harness)
          - harness:  A swappable agent harness
          - toolset:  A toolset of tools
          - agent:    An agent that uses tools
          - workflow: A workflow of agents

  -g, --group <GROUP>
          Group name to put the item in (prompted interactively if omitted)

      --image-name <IMAGE_NAME>
          The manifest `image_name`: the registry tag path leaf used at build time when `toolbox
          build --use-image-path` is set (image tools only; defaults to the item's name)

      --no-build
          Skip building this image in CI/CD (image already exists in registry; image tools only)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Skip interactive prompts and use defaults for all fields
          
          Interactive mode needs a terminal on stdin and stderr; without one, pass -n together with
          --group.

      --overwrite
          Overwrite existing files instead of skipping them

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox export (exit 0)
Export Thorium tools and pipelines into a toolbox directory structure

Reads tools (with every version's spec), pipelines, and with --all-kinds toolsets, agents, and
workflows from a running Thorium instance, and writes a toolbox directory with a manifest.toml per
item and a v2 toolbox.json ready to validate, diff, and import elsewhere. Container images stay in
their registry; model endpoints, profiles, and credentials are never exported.

Usage: thorctl toolbox export [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -g, --group <GROUP>
          Export everything of the selected kinds from this Thorium group (the source group to read
          from)
          
          Tools and pipelines are exported by default and `--all-kinds` adds toolsets, agents, and
          workflows. Exported items are written under this same group unless `--group-override` sets
          a different destination group. When re-exporting into an existing toolbox whose items live
          under a different group, `--overwrite` rewrites them in place to this group (no
          `--group-override` needed); without `--overwrite` a differing item is an error rather than
          a duplicate. With any of `--tools`, `--pipelines`, `--toolsets`, `--agents`, or
          `--workflows`, this is only the default group of the names given without one.

  -p, --pipelines <GROUP/NAME[=DIR]>
          Export specific pipelines. Format: `group/name[=dir]` (or `name[=dir]` with --group),
          comma-separated for multiple.
          
          Tools used by exported pipelines are auto-included. The optional `[=dir]` suffix writes
          that pipeline's files to `dir`, e.g. `static/av=pipelines/av`; without it the default
          layout (`pipelines/<group>/<name>`) is used. `dir` must be relative to the toolbox root
          and must not leave it. `[=dir]` is placement only; it never changes which pipeline (or its
          tools) is selected.

  -t, --tools <GROUP/NAME[=DIR]>
          Export specific tools. Format: `group/name[=dir]` (or `name[=dir]` with --group),
          comma-separated for multiple.
          
          Every version of a tool is exported (deprecated versions only with
          `--include-deprecated`). The optional `[=dir]` suffix writes that tool's files to `dir`,
          e.g. `static/clamav=tools/clamav`; naming an auto-included dependency tool this way also
          redirects it. `dir` must be relative to the toolbox root and must not leave it. `[=dir]`
          is placement only. `-i/--images` is a hidden alias of this flag for older scripts.

      --toolsets <GROUP/NAME[=DIR]>
          Export specific toolsets. Format: `group/name[=dir]` (or `name[=dir]` with --group),
          comma-separated for multiple.
          
          The tools a toolset includes are auto-included.

      --agents <GROUP/NAME[=DIR]>
          Export specific agents. Format: `group/name[=dir]` (or `name[=dir]` with --group),
          comma-separated for multiple.
          
          The harness, tools, toolsets, and sub-agents an agent names are auto-included.

      --workflows <GROUP/NAME[=DIR]>
          Export specific workflows. Format: `group/name[=dir]` (or `name[=dir]` with --group),
          comma-separated for multiple.
          
          The agents a workflow runs, and everything they name, are auto-included.

      --all-kinds
          With --group and no named items, also export the group's toolsets, agents, and workflows

      --include-deprecated
          Also export deprecated tool versions (by default they are skipped)
          
          A deprecated version that something pins (an agentic tool's container, a harness's
          provided tool) is exported anyway so the reference resolves.

      --latest-only
          Only export each tool's latest version
          
          A version that something pins is exported anyway so the reference resolves.

      --group-override <GROUP>
          Destination group: write every exported item under this group instead of its source group
          
          Use it to read from one Thorium group (`-g`/`--pipelines`/`--tools`) but store the items
          under a different group in the toolbox, or to align an export with an existing toolbox's
          group so the items reconcile and update in place. Two items of one kind that share a name
          can't both be exported, with or without this flag.

  -o, --output <DIR>
          Root directory for the exported toolbox
          
          Defaults to the `--config` directory when `--config` is given (so pointing at a toolbox's
          config.toml exports into that toolbox), otherwise `./toolbox` for a brand-new toolbox. An
          explicit value always wins; pass it to seed settings from one toolbox into a different
          output directory.

  -c, --config <CONFIG.TOML>
          Seed the toolbox-wide settings (name, registry, registries, `image_path_prefix`,
          `[base_image]`) from *another* toolbox's config.toml instead of --name/--registry
          
          This is for starting a new toolbox from an existing one's settings. Appending into a
          toolbox that already has a config.toml does NOT need this: an existing
          `<output>/config.toml` is auto-detected, reused, and preserved, and while it is kept it
          takes precedence over --config (which is then ignored with a warning). Settings-source
          priority is: kept `<output>/config.toml` > --config > --name/--registry. With
          --overwrite-config, an explicit --name/--registry is applied. Mutually exclusive with
          --name and --registry. Bundling is never inherited; it always follows --with-images.
          
          Giving --config also anchors --output to the config's directory unless --output is set, so
          `export -c mytb/config.toml ...` exports into `mytb/`. If the --config file doesn't exist,
          a warning is printed and a new toolbox is created there with the default name and no
          registry (--name/--registry can't be combined with --config).

      --name <NAME>
          Toolbox name for config.toml (default: "My Toolbox" for a new toolbox)
          
          Ignored with a warning while an existing config.toml is kept; applied over it with
          --overwrite-config.

      --registry <REGISTRY>
          Container registry for config.toml, e.g. ghcr.io/org/repo
          
          Optional: when omitted, the exported toolbox declares no central registry and relies on
          each image tool's own `image` url (captured on export unless `--strip-registry` is used).

      --skip-conflicts
          Skip on-disk conflicts: write new items and leave differing existing ones untouched with a
          warning (use --overwrite to overwrite instead)
          
          Without --overwrite or --skip-conflicts, an item that already exists in the output with
          different contents stops the export before anything is written.

      --review
          Open each exported item in an editor to review/tweak it before writing (requires a
          terminal)

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to open with --review (default: the configured editor, then $VISUAL, then
          $EDITOR)

      --overwrite
          Update a matched in-toolbox item that differs (otherwise the export stops listing it)
          
          When an item with the same kind and name already exists in the output and differs, this
          replaces it (its manifest.toml and spec files are rewritten and its toolbox.json entry is
          replaced); unchanged items are not rewritten. It does NOT touch config.toml; use
          --overwrite-config for that. With no selection and no --group, `--overwrite` refreshes
          every item already in the output from the same groups.

      --overwrite-config
          Overwrite an existing config.toml with this run's settings
          
          By default an existing config.toml is preserved (and its settings reused), so exporting
          into an existing toolbox never clobbers its settings. Pass this to replace it. Distinct
          from --overwrite, which covers per-item files.

      --with-images
          Bundle each image tool's container image file into the toolbox for offline transfer
          
          Pulls and saves each image tool's container with the configured container runtime (docker
          or podman, see --container-runtime) into its directory as `<dir>/<name>.tar.gz`. Only K8s
          image tools run from a container, so tools with other scalers are exported without a
          tarball; a K8s tool with no container url fails the export. Requires a container runtime.

      --strip-registry
          Write each image tool's container url as empty so the export carries no hard-coded
          registry path
          
          Clears the `image` url in every exported image tool's K8s environment, so a rebuild
          derives each image's path from the toolbox's own `config.toml`
          registry/`image_path_prefix` instead of a pinned url. Requires a registry in the effective
          config (--registry, --config, or the existing config.toml). **Conflicts with
          `--with-images`** (a bundled import needs the url to tag and push the saved tarball). A
          tool exported this way has a different spec hash than the tool in Thorium.

      --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox remove (exit 0)
Remove a previously imported toolbox from Thorium

Deletes the pipelines, workflows, agents, toolsets, and tools named by a toolbox manifest from the
target instance, in dependency order: pipelines, workflows, agents, and toolsets first, then each
tool's versions and the tool. Items that don't exist are reported and skipped, and a tool still used
by something outside the toolbox is left in place and reported. Groups and network policies are
never deleted.

Usage: thorctl toolbox remove [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox manifest to use: a toolbox.json path or an http(s) URL, or a toolbox
          directory. Format: `path | url | dir`
          
          A legacy toolbox is converted in memory first, so its images are removed as the tools an
          import created. A directory is read through its toolbox.json.

Options:
      --group-override <GROUP>
          Remove from this group instead of the groups recorded in the manifest
          
          Use this when the toolbox was imported with --group-override.

      --skip-confirm
          Skip the confirmation dialog
          
          The resources being removed are still listed before anything is deleted.
          
          [alias: --yes]

      --dry-run
          List what would be removed without deleting anything
          
          Needs no terminal, so it can preview a removal in CI.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox diff (exit 0)
Diff an on-disk toolbox against what a Thorium instance has imported

Shows what an import of this toolbox would change: each tool version is listed as new, unchanged
(counted only), or a label conflict, and toolsets, agents, workflows, and pipelines that differ show
unified hunks of their normalized definitions. A summary line counts each kind. Use --exit-code to
fail when an import would change something.

Usage: thorctl toolbox diff [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          A toolbox.json (path or http(s) URL) or a toolbox repo directory. Format: `path | url |
          dir`
          
          A legacy toolbox is converted in memory first, so its images are compared as the tools an
          import would create. A directory is read through its toolbox.json.

Options:
      --group-override <GROUP>
          Compare against this group instead of the groups recorded in the toolbox
          
          Use this when the toolbox was imported with --group-override.

      --exit-code
          Exit with code 1 when an import would change something (git diff --exit-code semantics)
          
          Counts tools that are new or gain a version, tool versions whose label exists with another
          spec, toolsets, agents, workflows, and pipelines that are new or differ, and differing
          network policies. Resources only in the instance never count, since an import doesn't
          delete them. Errors also exit with code 1, so exit code 1 alone doesn't distinguish drift
          from a failed diff.

      --image-path-prefix <REGISTRY/BASE>
          Target registry base path for images bundled in the toolbox
          
          Only used when the toolbox bundles container images (exported with `--with-images`). Each
          bundled image's url is compared as `<image-path-prefix>/<group>/<name>:<tag>`, the url an
          import with the same prefix would store. If omitted for a bundled toolbox, the prefix
          recorded in the manifest is used; with neither, urls are compared as-is. Never prompts.

      --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox build-images (exit 0)
Build (and optionally push) the container images in a toolbox

Walks a toolbox.json (a legacy images toolbox, or a v2 toolbox with tool versions that have a
`build` section and a `builds` section of base contexts) for entries with build enabled, builds each
entry's docker context with its first tag, aliases the remaining tags, and pushes all tags with
--push. Base contexts are built before the tools that start from them. For forks without CI: build,
push, then import as usual.

Usage: thorctl toolbox build-images [OPTIONS] [TOOLBOX.JSON]

Arguments:
  [TOOLBOX.JSON]
          The toolbox.json holding the build entries, or the directory holding it
          
          Reads either format: a v2 toolbox builds every tool version with a `build` section plus
          the `builds` section (base contexts other builds start from, built first), and a legacy
          toolbox builds its `images` entries. A directory is searched for `toolbox.json`, then
          `toolbox.v2.json`.
          
          [default: toolbox.json]

Options:
  -t, --tools <NAME>
          Only build these tools and base builds, comma-separated. Format: `name[,name,...]`
          (default: everything with build enabled)
          
          `-i/--images` is a hidden alias of this flag for older scripts.

      --dry-run
          Print what would be built, tagged, and pushed without running the container runtime
          
          Lists the entries in build order with their tags, build arguments, and the entries that
          are skipped, then exits.

      --push
          Push every built tag to its registry after building

      --base-image <ARG=IMAGE>
          Override an image's base image: `ARG=IMAGE`, passed to the build as `--build-arg
          ARG=IMAGE`
          
          Run-global escape hatch that overrides each image's resolved `[base_image]`. Only applied
          to images whose `[base_image].allow_override` is true (the default); images that opt out
          are built with their default base. `ARG` is the build arg the Dockerfile reads (e.g.
          `IMAGE` for `FROM ${IMAGE}`). On a key collision with a `--build-arg`, this value wins for
          images that allow the override.

      --build-arg <KEY=VALUE>
          Extra build arg passed to every image build: `KEY=VALUE` (repeatable)
          
          Unlike `--base-image`, these are passed to every build regardless of each entry's
          `base_image.allow_override` gate.

      --tag-suffix <SUFFIX>
          Append this suffix to every tag built and pushed, without touching toolbox.json
          
          Lets a CI/CD feature-branch run build/push differentiated images (e.g. `:1.0-mybranch`)
          from an unmodified toolbox.json so they don't collide with the mainline `:1.0`. Pass the
          separator you want (e.g. `-mybranch`).
          
          This is the alternative to `toolbox build --tag-suffix`, which bakes the suffix into
          toolbox.json instead; use one or the other. A tag that already ends with the suffix is not
          suffixed again.

  -e, --exit-on-error
          Stop at the first image whose build or push fails
          
          By default the build keeps going on failure — logging each one and exiting non-zero at the
          end if any image failed. This flag instead aborts the run on the first failure.

      --no-cache
          Build without the layer cache (passes `--no-cache` to docker/podman)
          
          Applies to every image built in this run.

      --pull
          Force a fresh pull of referenced images before building (passes `--pull` to docker/podman)
          
          Like docker/podman `--pull`, this refreshes every image the build references (including
          the `FROM` base) instead of using a locally cached one. Applies to every image built in
          this run.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox list (exit 0)
List what a toolbox manifest contains

Reads a toolbox manifest (a legacy or a v2 toolbox.json, a path, URL, or toolbox directory) and
lists its tools, toolsets, agents, workflows, pipelines, and build-only image contexts with their
groups and versions. Nothing is sent to Thorium.

Usage: thorctl toolbox list [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox to list. Format: `path | url | dir`

Options:
      --kind <KIND>
          Only list this kind of item

          Possible values:
          - tools:     Tools
          - toolsets:  Toolsets
          - agents:    Agents
          - workflows: Workflows
          - pipelines: Pipelines
          - builds:    Build-only image contexts

  -o, --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox validate (exit 0)
Check a toolbox manifest without talking to Thorium

Reads a toolbox manifest (a legacy or a v2 toolbox.json, a path, URL, or toolbox directory) and
reports every problem with it: unusable names, specs that don't parse, unresolved references,
dependency cycles, pipeline stages without a tool, and more. Exits with code 1 when there are
errors, or with --strict when there are warnings too.

Usage: thorctl toolbox validate [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox to check. Format: `path | url | dir`

Options:
      --strict
          Exit with code 1 on warnings and skipped items too, not only on errors

      --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox convert (exit 0)
Convert a legacy toolbox manifest to the current format

Reads a legacy toolbox (a toolbox.json with images and pipelines, given as a path, a URL, or a
toolbox directory) and writes the same toolbox as tools with their specs, bundled build contexts,
and pipelines whose stages name tools. Nothing is sent to Thorium and no login is needed. The input
is never changed unless --in-place is given.

By default the result is written to toolbox.v2.json next to the input. Images with no container
image are skipped, exactly as the migration of stored images skips them, and a pipeline with a stage
whose tool was not converted is left out; both are listed in the report together with every other
issue. A manifest that is already in the current format is accepted and converts to itself.

Usage: thorctl toolbox convert [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox to convert. Format: `path | url | dir`

Options:
  -o, --output <PATH | ->
          Where to write the converted manifest, or `-` for stdout (required for a URL input)

      --in-place
          Write the converted manifest over the input, first saving the input as `INPUT.bak`

      --no-backup
          Do not save a `.bak` copy of the input with --in-place (an existing `.bak` is an error
          without this flag)

      --check
          Write nothing and exit with code 1 when the converted manifest differs from the output
          file (or is missing it), printing a diff

      --dry-run
          Write nothing; print the report and, when the output file exists, a diff against it

      --report <FILE | ->
          Write the report as JSON to this file, or to stdout with `-`, instead of printing it on
          stderr

      --strict
          Write nothing and exit with code 1 when any item was skipped or any issue was raised

      --group-override <GROUP>
          Put every tool and pipeline in this group instead of the groups the legacy entries name

      --follow-latest
          Make pipeline stages follow their tool's latest version instead of pinning the version the
          legacy pipeline named

      --fill-image-from-tags
          Give a container image entry with no `image` the first of its build `image_tags` (default:
          skip the entry)

      --keep-incomplete-pipelines
          Keep a pipeline that has a stage with no converted tool, flagged `incomplete` (default:
          leave the pipeline out)

      --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

=== thorctl toolbox update (exit 0)
Refresh the items of a toolbox that were imported earlier

Works like import, but only touches the tools, toolsets, agents, workflows, and pipelines that
already exist in the target groups: nothing is created, no group is created, and the items that are
missing are listed as not imported. Use it to bring what you imported before up to date with a newer
copy of the toolbox.

Usage: thorctl toolbox update [OPTIONS] <PATH | URL | DIR>

Arguments:
  <PATH | URL | DIR>
          The toolbox manifest to use: a toolbox.json path, an http(s) URL, or a toolbox directory.
          Format: `path | url | dir`
          
          Legacy toolboxes (images and pipelines) are converted in memory first, exactly as `toolbox
          convert` would, and the conversion's issues are reported.

Options:
      --group-override <GROUP>
          Force every item to be imported into a specific group
          
          The group is created if it doesn't already exist.

  -t, --tools <NAME>
          Only import these tools (and what they need), comma separated
          
          Anything a selected item depends on is imported with it.

  -p, --pipelines <NAME>
          Only import these pipelines (and the tools they need), comma separated

      --only <KIND/NAME>
          Only import this item (and what it needs), written KIND/NAME; repeat for several items
          
          The kind is tool, toolset, agent, workflow, or pipeline.

      --overwrite
          Apply every difference without asking
          
          A changed tool version is published under its label, or under `LABEL+HASH` when the label
          is taken by a different spec, since published versions never change. Toolsets, agents,
          workflows, and pipelines that differ are updated in place.

      --skip-conflicts
          Create what is missing but never change what already exists
          
          Each existing item that differs from the toolbox is left as it is and listed. This is safe
          for non-interactive (CI or agent) imports that must never overwrite local changes.

      --dry-run
          Show what would be created, published, and updated, and write nothing

      --skip-confirm
          Don't ask for confirmation before writing
          
          [alias: --yes]

  -o, --format <FORMAT>
          The format to print in: table, human, json (the API's response), or yaml

          Possible values:
          - table: A table with one row per item
          - human: Labeled fields for people to read
          - json:  The API's response as JSON
          - yaml:  The same document as the JSON format, written as YAML

      --rollback-on-failure
          Automatically roll back what was applied if the import stops early
          
          Without this flag a session with a terminal is asked, and any other session keeps what was
          applied and reports it.

      --editor <EDITOR>
          The editor to use when reviewing a difference
          
          Accepted for compatibility; differences are decided with Apply, Skip, or Quit.

      --update-network-policy
          Update existing Thorium network policies to match the toolbox
          
          Without this, an existing policy that differs is left untouched and only a warning is
          logged. With it, the toolbox's definition is authoritative: rule/flag differences
          overwrite the policy, and a groups-only difference adds the toolbox's groups to the
          existing policy. Group coverage is only ever added, never removed.
          
          This overwrites cluster network-security state, and it applies WITHOUT a prompt when the
          session can't ask.

      --image-path-prefix <REGISTRY/BASE>
          Target registry base path for images bundled in the toolbox
          
          Only used when the toolbox bundles container images (exported with `--with-images`): each
          tool's container image is rewritten to `<image-path-prefix>/<group>/<name>:<tag>`.
          Importing does not push container images; push them to that registry first.

      --follow-latest
          Make pipeline stages of a legacy toolbox follow their tool's latest version instead of
          pinning the version the legacy pipeline named

      --no-pipelines
          Don't import pipelines

      --strict
          Stop before any write if converting a legacy toolbox raised an issue or the manifest has a
          warning

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
